# enfilade — the east and west connecting passages, both floors: the porcelain cabinet on the
#            ground storey and the picture gallery on the piano nobile.
#
# ================================================================================================
# WHAT THIS PART PUTS UNDER LOAD — do not "tidy" any of it away. Every one of these is a question
# put to the path tracer, and each is the reason a piece of this room is shaped the way it is
# rather than a way that would have been easier.
#
# 1. THE NARROWEST WALKABLE SPACE IN THE BUILDING, WITH A MIRROR DOWN ONE SIDE OF IT.
#
#    1.15 m in the clear between a `mirror` wall at x = 13.95 and a window wall at x = 15.10, and
#    6.05 m long, twice on each side of the building. `mirror` is rough=6, metal=252, and the whole
#    west wall of the lower passage carries it from the dado cap at 2.70 to the cornice at 4.50 —
#    10.9 m2 of it per passage, 21.8 m2 in the building, facing a wall of glass 1.15 m away.
#
#    That is a different rig from the mirror salon below and from the ballroom above, and the
#    difference is the whole point of building it. Those two put mirror opposite mirror across a
#    room 3.15 m and 2.97 m wide; the path bounces at a steep angle and dies in a few hops. Here
#    the facing surface is a WINDOW, the gap is 1.15 m, and every path that enters at anything but
#    normal incidence bounces between the two at a SHALLOW ANGLE and keeps bouncing — which is the
#    case where a bounce budget is spent fastest and where any error in the mirror's normal is
#    multiplied by every bounce after it. A wide room hides that. This cannot.
#
#    IT WAS 0.90 AND IT IS NOW 1.15, WHICH IS EVERY MILLIMETRE THERE IS. See THE WIDTH below: the
#    strip is what halls.clip's block face at 13.95 and walls.clip's inner face at 15.10 leave, and
#    the two linings are now 0.15 of my own stone standing INSIDE those two neighbours rather than
#    beside them. The shallow-angle rig is unchanged in kind and 28% wider in fact.
#
#    IT IS BROKEN BY THE SHELVES AND BY WHAT STANDS ON THEM, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. Three shelf
#    runs cross the mirror at 2.925, 3.375 and 3.825, four gilt strips divide it on the window jamb
#    lines, and about a hundred and fifty vessels stand in front of it. An unbroken mirror facing a
#    window is a degenerate corridor and tells you nothing; interrupted at every bounce by real
#    geometry, the recursion carries something to look at. salon.clip argued exactly this and it is
#    the same argument.
#
# 2. HUNDREDS OF SMALL TRANSLUCENT CLEARCOATED BODIES, CLOSE TO THE EYE.
#
#    `porcelain` is translucent=90 AND lacquer=12. A glazed white body is both at once and a
#    single-lobe shader cannot fake it; nothing else in this project carries both keys. There are
#    about 150 porcelain and faience vessels in each passage — 25 jars and 23 beakers to each of
#    three shelf runs, six plates, four large jars on the pier brackets — 0.06 to 0.225 m across,
#    three hundred and eight in the building.
#
#    IN A PASSAGE 1.15 m WIDE THE NEAREST OF THEM IS 0.45 m FROM THE CAMERA. That is where
#    subsurface and clearcoat are judged and where nothing can be hidden by distance. It is also
#    why they are small: a big translucent body at arm's length is a lighting fixture, a small one
#    is a test of whether the two lobes are separable. `faience` (lacquer=10, no translucency) is
#    on the beakers beside them for the same reason halls.clip put two panel depths in one room —
#    the comparison is what makes either of them a measurement.
#
# 3. A DEEP REVEAL: A 1.80 m TUNNEL OF STONE PER WINDOW, AND A BEAT DOWN THE LENGTH.
#
#    windows.clip cuts its east lights 1.80 m in from x = 16.00. Three of them stand in this
#    passage on each floor, on the 2.70 m eustyle bay at z = 0 and +-2.70, and each is a shaft:
#    1.05 m of stone from the outer face to my finished face at 14.95, then 0.75 m more of open
#    cut before the light reaches the room. The passage is therefore a row of three bright slots
#    with almost-black between them, on a KNOWN PITCH, which is the highest-contrast repeating
#    pattern in the building. Any per-bay drift in the indirect term shows as a beat down the
#    length, and the two passages on each floor are the same room twice, so the second one is the
#    control.
#
# 4. BUSTS: IRREGULAR MASSES IN SHALLOW NICHES.
#
#    Five round-headed niches to a gallery, each 0.45 wide and 0.225 deep with a hemispherical
#    head, each with a bust of `alabaster` (translucent=210) or `marble` (translucent=110) on a
#    `giallo` or `rosso` socle. A bust in a niche is the soft-gradient case — a rounded mass lit
#    only by what the niche's own curved back throws onto it — and there are five in a row, on
#    1.35 m centres, alternating in both material pairs, so one can be compared against the next
#    INSIDE ONE FRAME rather than between two renders.
#
# 5. AND THE EMISSIVES. Six ormolu girandoles in the cabinet and eight sconces in the gallery,
#    each with `wax` tapers (translucent=150) and `taper` flames (emit=210), 20 flames to a
#    passage side and forty in the building. In the cabinet they are the only light after dark on a wall of mirror; in the
#    gallery they are at eye height, 1.70 m above the floor, raking along the pictures.
# ================================================================================================
#
# THE MODULE, AND THE ONE NUMBER THAT IS INHERITED RATHER THAN CHOSEN.
#
# M = 0.45, D = 0.90.
#
# THE WIDTH OF THIS PART IS NOT A DECISION AND IT IS NOW ALL OF IT. The strip is what the built
# neighbours left: halls.clip stops its block at x = 13.95 and walls.clip's inner face is at
# x = 15.10, so there are 1.15 m between them and nothing can widen it.
#
# IT USED TO SPEND 0.25 OF THAT ON ITS OWN LININGS AND NOW IT SPENDS NONE. The finished faces stood
# at 14.05 and 14.95 — 0.90 = 2 M in the clear — with 0.15 of lining inside each neighbour's face.
# They now stand ON those two faces, 13.95 and 15.10, and the linings have gone the other way: my
# block runs 13.80 .. 15.20, which is 0.15 INTO halls.clip's block and 0.10 INTO walls.clip's ring,
# both of which are solid stone there for the whole height of this part. So each lining is still
# 0.15 thick — which is what keeps this part one solid on its own at any metre — and none of it is
# in the room any more. Nothing is removed from either neighbour: `enf_air` stops at 13.95 and
# 15.10, so the void the manifest subtracts never reaches their stone.
#
# 1.15 IS 0.05 SHORT OF THE 1.20 A CIRCULATION ROUTE WANTS AND THAT CANNOT BE FIXED FROM THIS FILE.
# The two numbers that bound it are halls.clip's 13.95 and walls.clip's 15.10. Moving either is that
# file's line and a request, not something to do quietly from here.
#
#   PLAN
#    13.60   back of the upper west wall. See WHY THE UPPER WEST WALL IS THICKER, below.
#    13.80   back of the lower west lining, 0.15 into halls.clip's block face at 13.95
#    13.95   FINISHED WEST FACE, both floors — halls.clip's own block face
#    13.995  face of the west dado cap, skirting, base course, strips and frames, 0.045 (M/10) proud
#    14.0625 front of a shelf, M/4 proud
#    14.10   front of a corbel under one
#    15.00   windows.clip's `wall_cut` limit, and THE LINE NOTHING OF MINE MAY CROSS inside a
#            window's own y and z bands — see WHAT DELETES WORK IN THIS STRIP, below. It was 14.20
#            until the cutter was fixed to the thickness of the wall it cuts.
#    14.525  centre line of the passage — the middle of what the neighbours left, and not a module
#            number, for the reason THE WIDTH gives
#    15.055  face of the east dado cap, skirting, base course, impost and architraves
#    15.10   FINISHED EAST FACE, both floors — walls.clip's own inner face
#    15.20   0.10 into walls.clip's ring, which is solid 15.10 .. 16.00
#    -2.95   south face   salon.clip and chapel.clip below, ballroom.clip and library.clip above,
#                         and all four of them stand their north face on -2.95 / -3.00
#    -3.00   0.05 lap into them
#     3.10   north face
#     3.15   0.05 lap into the stair well; stair.clip's own south face is 3.15 = 7 M
#     0, +-2.70   the window axes. windows.clip's, on the contract's eustyle bay, and the spine
#                 this part is set out on: every bay division, girandole, niche and picture frame
#                 is on one of them or on the M/5 grid between two.
#
#   SECTION, the porcelain cabinet
#    1.75   founding, 0.05 into the podium         2.925 3.375 3.825   the three shelf tops,
#    1.80   FLOOR                    podium_top          2.5 M, 3.5 M and 4.5 M above the floor
#    1.98   skirting head            1.80 + 0.4 M   4.05  girandole band foot   1.80 + 5 M
#    2.565  dado body head                          4.50  cornice soffit = the window head
#    2.70   dado cap top   1.80 + 2 M = the sill    4.725 cornice top / cove springing
#                                                   4.95  ceiling soffit        1.80 + 7 M
#                                                   6.00  top of the lower zone
#
#   SECTION, the picture gallery
#    6.30   FLOOR   the piano nobile                7.875 niche crown           7.65 + M/2
#    6.75   base course top / niche floor  + M      8.17  head of the tallest thing on the wall,
#    7.20   socle top                     + 2 M           a sconce flame, whose centre is at 8.11
#    7.65   niche springing               + 3 M     8.55  window head (windows.clip's own)
#                                                   8.685 impost foot
#                                                   8.775 VAULT SPRINGING       6.30 + 5.5 M
#                                                   8.70  head of the south doorway, which is
#                                                         ballroom.clip's and library.clip's own
#                                                   9.35  vault crown           8.775 + 1.15/2
#                                                   9.90  top of the block      column_head
#
#   THE SPRINGING WENT UP FROM 8.55 TO 8.775 AND IT IS THE HEAD HEIGHT THAT PUT IT THERE. A barrel
#   sprung on the window head gave 2.25 m at the wall face, which is over the brief's 2.10 and under
#   the 2.40 a circulation route wants. Sprung at 8.775 — 5.5 M over the floor — the clear height at
#   both wall faces is 2.475 and 3.05 on the centre line, and the whole vault is still ABOVE
#   windows.clip's cut at 8.55, so the manifest takes nothing of it. The impost moved with it, to
#   8.685 .. 8.775, which is 2.385 m over the floor and out of the head band; and because it is now
#   entirely above 8.55 it no longer has to be cut back to the window jambs and runs straight.
#
#   TOLERANCES, never seen and never measured: the 0.05 laps (1.75, 13.90, 15.15, -3.00, 3.15,
#   6.25), the 0.02 and 0.035 under a paint rule's `below`, and the small rounds on every arris.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT DELETES WORK IN THIS STRIP, AND IT DOES IT SILENTLY
#
# windows.clip's east openings are cut `wall_cut` = 1.00 m in from x = 16.00, which lands at
# x = 15.00 — 0.10 m INSIDE my finished east face. `void_windows` is subtracted from the whole
# building in the manifest, so ANY solid of mine standing at x > 15.00, inside a window's y band and
# inside its z band, is deleted after assembly with no error, no warning and no change to any count.
#
# IT USED TO BE 1.80 AND IT LANDED AT 14.20, WHICH IS 0.90 m INSIDE THIS ROOM. That is the number
# this file was written round: the shelves were M/4 deep to clear it, the girandoles' branches were
# short to clear it, and the east base course and impost were cut back to the window jambs by hand
# because the manifest would otherwise have left them ragged. windows.clip's own wall is 0.90 thick
# and its cutter is now 1.00 — 0.90 of wall plus walls.clip's own 0.10 of cutter slack — so the line
# has moved 0.80 m east and every one of those constraints has slack in it that it did not have.
# The members are left where they are: nothing above is wrong now, it is merely no longer forced.
#
#   the bands, measured off windows_gs_cut / windows_pn_cut and not assumed:
#      y 2.70 .. 4.50   ground storey        y 6.30 .. 8.55   piano nobile
#      z -3.375 .. -2.025,  -0.675 .. 0.675,  2.025 .. 3.375  (the three lights, 1.35 wide)
#
# Everything in this file is placed against those six numbers:
#
#   - THE SHELVES ARE M/4 DEEP AND NOT M/2, and that is now a decision about a passage 1.15 m wide
#     rather than about a cutter. At 0.1125 the front is 14.0625, which leaves 1.0375 m to walk in.
#   - THE VESSELS ARE 0.10 AND 0.06 ACROSS, the widest of them reaching 14.065.
#   - THE GIRANDOLES AND SCONCES REACH 14.045 AND NO FURTHER, which is now a walking clearance
#     and not a cutter clearance: a sconce's flame is 1.81 m over the gallery floor, which is
#     shoulder height in a passage 1.15 m wide, and it is what decides the width of `enf_walk_u`.
#   - THE THINGS THAT PROJECT FROM THE EAST WALL are on the two piers at z +-1.35, between the
#     windows, where no cut can reach at any x: the bracket jars stand out 0.225 there.
#   - THE EAST BASE COURSE IS CUT BACK TO THE WINDOW JAMBS BY THIS FILE, so the interruption is a
#     clean return and not a ragged end left by somebody else's difference. The impost no longer
#     needs it: at 8.685 it is above the cut altogether.
#   - THE WINDOW ARCHITRAVES ARE SIZED BY THE SAME SIX NUMBERS. Their jambs stand at z 0.6825, which
#     is 7.5 mm outside the opening; the upper head sits at 8.55, on the cut's own ceiling; the lower
#     sill at 2.61 .. 2.70, under its floor. Not one voxel of them is inside the band.
#
# AND BOTH ENDS OF BOTH PASSAGES ARE OPEN FOR THE FULL SECTION, WHICH IS NOT A CHOICE EITHER.
# The outer two lights at z = +-2.70 run z -3.375..-2.025 and 2.025..3.375, and my passage runs
# -2.95..3.10 — so both of them STRADDLE MY OWN END FACES. Any end wall I build is taken out from
# x 14.20 eastwards over the whole opening height. So the ends are archways rather than doors:
# their heads are the cornice at 4.50 below and the vault crown at 8.55 above, which is 2.70 m and
# 2.25 m of clear opening against the brief's 2.40 for a door, and the south doorway is carried on
# to 8.70 to match the 2.40 the ballroom and the library cut on their own side of it.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THE UPPER WEST WALL IS THICKER, AND WHY IT IS THE ONE THING THAT LEAVES THE ZONE
#
# On the ground storey the west side of this strip is halls.clip's block, solid from 1.75 to 6.975,
# so the wall there is a LINING 0.15 thick bedded 0.05 into it at 13.90. Above 6.975 there is
# nothing to the west at all — the upper east hall is unbuilt — so the gallery has to bring its own
# wall, and a wall with a 0.225 niche cut into it needs a back. So the upper west wall is M thick,
# 13.60 .. 14.05, which leaves M/2 of stone behind every niche; it stands on halls.clip's block
# over 13.60..13.95 and on my own floor slab east of that. That is 0.45 m west of the zone the
# brief gave, it is the only thing in this file outside it, and it is buried: nothing of it can be
# seen from either passage or from anywhere else in the building.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT I COULD NOT MAKE WORK, SAID PLAINLY. Three things, all of them written up with their measurements in
# clips/facility/requests/enfilade.md, and neither is fixable from inside this file:
#
#   1. THE PIANO NOBILE IS STILL NOT CONTINUOUS. stair.clip's gallery is a floor at 6.30 from
#      z = 4.515 north; my gallery's north doorway is at z = 3.10. Between them, x 14.05..15.10 by
#      z 3.10..4.515, THERE IS NO FLOOR — it is the open well over the lower flight. A person
#      leaving the ballroom reaches the north end of my gallery and stops there. Bridging it means
#      taking out stair.clip's own south balustrade at z = 4.59, which is that file's line and not
#      mine, so it is not attempted here.
#   2. THE VAULT HAS NO LUNETTES. The brief asked for a barrel springing at 8.10 with a lunette
#      over each window; a lunette is stone between a window head and a vault, windows.clip deletes
#      every piece of stone below 8.55 within 1.80 m of the outer wall, and the measurement is in
#      THE AIR OF THE GALLERY below. The vault springs at 8.55 instead, which is better walkable
#      and costs nothing else.
#   3. THE GROUND-FLOOR SOUTH DOORWAY IS PREPARED AND NOT OPEN. salon.clip's north wall runs
#      z -3.36..-2.95 and chapel.clip's north face is -2.95, and neither cuts a north doorway. My
#      air laps the brief's 0.05 into them and stops; 0.36 m of their wall still stands in it. The
#      cabinet is entered from its north end, which is open to the passage under the stair.
#
# The ground passage IS reachable and always was: its north end opens on the 22 m2 of floor under
# the stair, at 1.80, which runs west to the rotunda.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MEASURED, with clips/facility/requests/enfilade-probe.clip, which restates `bounds` as the two
# strips plus a margin so a metre 32 run is seven seconds rather than minutes.
#
#   metre 32   TWO COMPONENTS, which is the answer that matters, and the same answer as before
#   metre 16   TWO, once the jar's neck and lid were thickened — see NOTHING FREE-STANDING, below
#   metre  8   EIGHT: the two passages and six one- and two-voxel fragments of a bust's head and a
#              girandole's flame, which are 0.19 and 0.06 across against a voxel of 0.125
#
# AND ONE THING THE WALKING BAND COST, SAID PLAINLY. On the whole building at metre 8, `enf_wax_face`
# and `enf_fire_face` now report `never matched`: the forty tapers and flames are 0.052 and 0.06
# across, and pulling them back from 14.085 to 14.045 to clear `enf_walk_u` moved them off the
# sample column they used to happen to land on. They are there at metre 16 and at the contract's
# metre 32 — the probe reports no unmatched rule at either — and at metre 8 a flame half a voxel
# wide is sampled by luck rather than by geometry. Getting them back means letting something reach
# past 14.05, which is the whole of the 1.00 m band. The band is worth more than the luck.
#
# TWO IS THE RIGHT ANSWER AND ONE IS NOT AVAILABLE. They are the two passages, east and west;
# `--part enf_east` — one side, both floors, everything in them — reports ONE component with
# NOTHING FLOATING at metre 32, and the west copy is the same 1,210,578 voxels to the voxel, which
# is what tells you the fold happened. halls.clip reports two for the same reason and says so.
#
# 78 AT METRE 8 IS THE GEOMETRY BEING FINER THAN THE GRID AND NOT A FAULT. Every one of the 76
# extra pieces is a girandole's or a sconce's arm, taper and flame, which are 0.052 across
# and under half a voxel at 0.125 m. By metre 16 they have all knitted and the count is 2. Judge
# this part at 32, which is the contract's own metre.
#
# THE PROBE'S OWN `enf_head_*`, `enf_door_*` AND `enf_check_*` BOXES ARE STALE. Every one of them
# is written to the old faces at 14.05 and 14.85, so they now test a slice of open room rather than
# the clearance they were built for. They are in requests/enfilade-probe.clip, which is not this
# file; the boxes want moving to 13.95 / 14.95 and the head boxes want widening to 14.09 .. 15.06.
#
# CLEAR HEIGHT, `--gap y@x,z` on the probe at metre 8, which works here because the probe holds only
# this part and each floor is closed by my own slab above and below it. The columns are named by the
# voxel their x falls in, because that is what is actually sampled:
#
#                                                    before   after
#   14.0625, the first voxel inside the west wall     1.875    2.875
#   14.1875, one further in                           3.125    3.125
#   14.5625, the centre line                          3.125    3.125
#   14.9375, the last voxel before the east trim      3.125    3.125
#
# The before reading at 14.0625 came back flagged `-- UNDER THE 2.10 m HEAD HEIGHT` by clipcheck
# itself: that column is the gallery's vault haunch over its own base course, and it is exactly the
# place a barrel vault fails and the middle of the room does not.
#
# CLEAR WIDTH is `enf_walk_g` and `enf_walk_u` above and not a `--gap`, for the reason written
# there: 1.00 m by 2.10 m, the whole length, both floors, EMPTY at metre 32. What the passage is in
# the clear is 1.15 m; 1.06 m past the dado cap and the base course cap, which stand 0.045 proud
# either side; 1.033 m past the widest jar on the shelves.
#
#   the doorways  north, both floors: the whole clear section, 1.15 m — they are archways and their
#                 heads are the cornice at 4.50 and the vault crown at 8.775
#                 south, gallery: 0.90 m, which is ballroom.clip's and library.clip's own cut and
#                 not mine, and was 0.80 before this room's west face moved on to 13.95
#                 south, cabinet: 1.00 m in my own wall, and still blind — salon.clip and
#                 chapel.clip have not opened their side of it
#
# Things I got wrong and the probe had to tell me, all of them written where they happened: every
# moulding was drawn from inside the wall to its full projection and each left a strip one voxel
# square and 185 long floating at its arris; the pier brackets were at chest height in a passage
# 0.90 wide; the sconces overlapped the picture frames; and the vault sprang at 8.10, where
# windows.clip quietly ate 4171 voxels of its haunches.

include "_contract.clip"

# ================================================================================================
# THE TWO BLOCKS — solid masses that the passages are cut out of
# ================================================================================================
#
# Written as blocks with holes in them, the way halls.clip writes a vaulted room, because that is
# what makes each floor certainly one solid before anything is added to it. The lower block laps
# 0.05 into the podium at 1.75, 0.05 into halls.clip at 13.90 and 0.05 into walls.clip at 15.15.
# The floor slab between them carries the piano nobile at 6.30 and is the cabinet's ceiling slab.

let enf_block_g = box 13.80 1.75 -2.95   15.20 6.00 3.10 round=0.015
let enf_slab    = box 13.60 6.00 -2.95   15.20 6.30 3.10 round=0.015
let enf_block_u = box 13.60 6.25 -2.95   15.20 9.90 3.10 round=0.015

# ================================================================================================
# THE AIR OF THE CABINET — floor to cornice, then a cove to a flat ceiling
# ================================================================================================
#
# The room is 0.90 x 2.70 in the clear to the cornice soffit at 4.50, which is windows.clip's own
# window head, so the cornice IS the head of every light and nothing has to be interrupted.
#
# THE COVE IS A ROUNDED BOX AND NOT FOUR MOULDINGS, for the reason salon.clip gives: a box grown by
# r and rounded by the same r is exactly that solid of revolution, and four cavettos mitred at the
# corners would leave four seams in the one surface here that has none. The core box's TOP FACE is
# the flat ceiling at 4.95 and its own extent, 14.365..14.635, is the width of that flat — 0.27,
# which is 0.6 M and all a 0.90 passage can spare once M/2 of cove is taken off each side.
#
# It is clipped in z to the block faces so that the growth cannot reach past them into the stair
# well or into the salon's wall; the core box is run 0.15 long at each end so the clip lands well
# inside the rounding and the cove does not pinch at the last 0.10 m.

let enf_g_room = box 13.95 1.80 -2.95   15.10 4.50 3.10
let enf_g_ceil = intersection {
    round { box 14.175 4.50 -3.10   14.875 4.725 3.25 } by=0.225
    box 13.95 4.50 -2.95   15.10 4.95 3.10
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE AIR OF THE GALLERY — a barrel vault springing at 8.55
# ================================================================================================
#
# The barrel is a semicircle of M struck on the centre line: it springs at 8.55 off both finished
# faces — windows.clip's own piano-nobile head — and crowns at 9.00, which is 6.30 + 6 M.
#
# THE BRIEF ASKED FOR IT TO SPRING AT 8.10 WITH A LUNETTE OVER EVERY WINDOW, AND IT CANNOT. Both
# halves of that were measured before they were given up, and the measurement is
# `--part enf_win_u_check` in requests/enfilade-probe.clip:
#
#   A LUNETTE IS THE STONE BETWEEN A WINDOW HEAD AND A VAULT, and windows.clip's piano-nobile cut
#   is a RECTANGLE 1.35 wide running to y = 8.55 and 1.80 m in from the outer face, which lands at
#   x = 14.20 — 0.75 m inside this room. So every piece of stone that a lunette could be made of is
#   inside that box and is deleted by the manifest. Sprung at 8.10 the vault crowns at 8.55, its
#   haunches stand between the soffit and that plane, and the check came back with 4171 voxels in
#   it: the west haunch of all three window bays, planed flat at 8.55, silently.
#
#   AND SPRINGING AT 8.10 IS 1.80 m OVER THE FLOOR, which is head height at the wall face and
#   2.10 m only within 0.335 of the centre line. halls.clip raised its own springing by one module
#   for exactly this and wrote down what it cost: 0.45 of stone above, and a room that went from
#   squat to properly proportioned at the same time.
#
# Sprung at 8.55 the whole vault is ABOVE the cut, so the manifest takes nothing of it; the clear
# height is 2.25 at both wall faces and 2.70 at the crown; the window head lands exactly on the
# springing line, which is what an impost is for; and the 2.40 m doorway the ballroom and the
# library cut at the south end, head 8.70, passes under a soffit that is 8.82 at its west jamb and
# 8.83 at its east. The lunettes are the price and they are written up in requests/enfilade.md.

let enf_u_room  = box 13.95 6.30 -2.95   15.10 8.775 3.10
let enf_u_barrel = intersection {
    cylinder 14.525 8.775 0.075  r=0.575 h=6.15 axis=z
    box 13.95 8.775 -2.95   15.10 9.35 3.10
}
let enf_u_vault = enf_u_barrel

# ================================================================================================
# THE FOUR OPENINGS, AND THE 0.05 LAP INTO WHAT IS ON THE OTHER SIDE OF EACH
# ================================================================================================
#
# North, both floors: the stair well, whose south face is stair.clip's 3.15. The opening is the
# whole clear section — see the note at the head of this file on why it cannot be anything else —
# and its head is the cornice at 4.50 below and the vault crown at 8.55 above.
#
# South, both floors: 14.05..14.85. The east half of that is my own room and the west half is the
# 0.90 doorway ballroom.clip cuts at x 13.95..14.85 and library.clip cuts at the mirrored -14.85..
# -13.95, both 2.40 high from 6.30 to 8.70. So the gallery's south doorway is 0.80 m wide in the
# clear and 2.40 m high, which is the brief's door, and it is on the line the two rooms already
# opened rather than on a line of my own. The cabinet's south doorway is the same 0.80 at ground
# level; salon.clip and chapel.clip have not opened their side of it, which is written up at the
# head of this file and in requests/enfilade.md.

let enf_dr_gs = box 13.95 1.80 -3.00   14.95 4.50 -2.90
let enf_dr_gn = box 13.95 1.80  3.00   15.10 4.50  3.15
let enf_dr_us = box 13.95 6.30 -3.00   14.85 8.70 -2.90
let enf_dr_un = box 13.95 6.30  3.00   15.10 8.70  3.15

# ================================================================================================
# THE NICHES — five to a gallery, on 1.35 (3 M) centres, opposite the windows and the piers
# ================================================================================================
#
# 0.45 (M) wide, 0.225 (M/2) deep, floor at 6.75 on the base course, springing at 7.65 and crowned
# at 7.875 by a hemisphere of the same radius — a half cylinder and a half sphere, which is what a
# round-headed niche is and what gives the bust in it a CURVED back to be lit by.
#
# They are the deepest cut in this file and they are all on the west wall, at x < 14.20, so no
# window reveal can reach them whatever z they stand on. That is why the niches are opposite the
# lights and the pictures are not: a bust wants the window and a hanging does not.

let enf_niche_one = union {
    cylinder 13.95 7.20 0  r=0.225 h=0.90 axis=y
    sphere   13.95 7.65 0  r=0.225
}
let enf_niches = intersection {
    translate { repeat { enf_niche_one } z=1.35 nz=2 } 0 0 0
    box 13.60 6.75 -2.95   13.95 7.90 3.10
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE SUNK PICTURE PANELS AND THE WINDOW REBATES
# ================================================================================================
#
# Four panels to a gallery, between the niches on the window jamb lines at z = +-0.675 and +-2.025,
# 0.36 wide and 0.90 (2 M) tall, sunk 0.03 — one voxel at the contract's metre 32, the depth
# salon.clip sinks its own fields at and for the same reason: the relief is CUT and the hanging in
# it is PAINTED, and having both in one wall is what makes either of them a fair question.
#
# One more on each east pier, 0.90 wide, in the same band.

let enf_pan_seed = box 13.92 6.975 -0.18   13.95 7.875 0.18
let enf_pan_w = union {
    translate { enf_pan_seed } 0 0 -0.675
    translate { enf_pan_seed } 0 0  0.675
    translate { enf_pan_seed } 0 0 -2.025
    translate { enf_pan_seed } 0 0  2.025
}
let enf_pan_e = union {
    box 15.10 6.975 -1.80   15.13 7.875 -0.90
    box 15.10 6.975  0.90   15.13 7.875  1.80
}
let enf_panels = union { enf_pan_w enf_pan_e }

# A REBATE ROUND EACH LIGHT WAS THE OLD ANSWER AND IT DIED WITH THE WIDENING. It was a step cut
# back into the east lining, and the east lining is now 0.05 thick: the finished face stands on
# walls.clip's own inner plane at 15.10, so there is nothing left of mine to sink a rebate into.
# What replaces it is the better member anyway — AN ARCHITRAVE, standing 0.045 proud of the face and
# 0.09 wide, on the jambs of every light and across the head of the upper ones.
#
# EVERY PIECE OF IT IS OUTSIDE THE BAND windows.clip DELETES, and that is what decides its size.
# The cut is z -0.675..0.675 about each axis and y 2.70..4.50 below, 6.30..8.55 above, from x = 14.20
# eastward. So the jambs stand at z 0.6825..0.7725 — 7.5 mm clear of the opening — the upper head sits
# at 8.55..8.64, on the cut's own ceiling, and the lower sill at 2.61..2.70, under its floor. The
# lower light needs no head because the cornice at 4.50 already is one, which is the reason the
# cornice is at 4.50.
let enf_arc_g_jamb = mirror { box 15.055 2.61 0.6825   15.20 4.50 0.7725 round=0.008 } axis=z
let enf_arc_g_sill = box 15.055 2.61 -0.7725   15.20 2.70 0.7725 round=0.008
let enf_arc_g_seed = union { enf_arc_g_jamb enf_arc_g_sill }

let enf_arc_u_jamb = mirror { box 15.055 6.75 0.6825   15.20 8.64 0.7725 round=0.008 } axis=z
let enf_arc_u_head = box 15.055 8.55 -0.7725   15.20 8.64 0.7725 round=0.008
let enf_arc_u_seed = union { enf_arc_u_jamb enf_arc_u_head }

let enf_architraves = union {
    translate { enf_arc_g_seed } 0 0 -2.70
    enf_arc_g_seed
    translate { enf_arc_g_seed } 0 0  2.70
    translate { enf_arc_u_seed } 0 0 -2.70
    enf_arc_u_seed
    translate { enf_arc_u_seed } 0 0  2.70
}

# THERE IS NO DOORCASE ON EITHER SOUTH OPENING AND THERE CANNOT BE ONE FROM THIS FILE. It was built
# and taken out again, which is worth writing down: a doorcase stands on the wall beside and over an
# opening, and THIS PART HAS NO SOUTH WALL. My block's south face and my room's south face are both
# at -2.95; what the hole is cut through is ballroom.clip's north wall on the piano nobile and
# salon.clip's on the ground storey. A case drawn at z -2.995..-2.90 came out as a pilaster and a
# lintel standing in mid-air 0.16 m clear of my own east wall, attached to nothing of mine —
# invisible in the assembled building, where the neighbour's stone happens to be behind it, and
# four floating components in `--part part_enfilade`. The south doorcases belong to the two files
# that own the walls. The twelve window architraves above are this file's architraves.

# ================================================================================================
# THE SHELL
# ================================================================================================

let enf_air = union {
    enf_g_room enf_g_ceil enf_u_room enf_u_vault
    enf_dr_gs enf_dr_gn enf_dr_us enf_dr_un
    enf_niches
}
let enf_cuts  = union { enf_air enf_panels }
let enf_shell = difference { union { enf_block_g enf_slab enf_block_u } enf_cuts }

# ================================================================================================
# THE TRIM OF THE CABINET — skirting, dado, cornice
# ================================================================================================
#
# Every run stops at z = -2.85 and 2.95, which is 0.10 short of each end face and 0.05 clear of the
# doorway boxes above. THAT IS THE WHOLE OF WHY IT IS WRITTEN AS RUNS AND NOT AS FRAMES: a frame is
# the difference of two boxes and it returns across both ends, and salon.clip measured 822 voxels
# of its own skirting, dado cap and cornice standing straight across its own doorway — three bars
# at ankle, waist and head height — which nothing in a component count, a volume or a material list
# will ever report. `intersection { part_enfilade <the opening> }` is the only thing that finds it,
# and there are four of them checked in requests/enfilade-probe.clip's own notes.
#
# Every moulding starts INSIDE the wall it stands on — 0.045 for the curved members — for the
# reason walls.clip gives: a section that starts on the face leaves a hairline for the manifest's
# 12 mm of grain to open. And no member projects less than 0.045: vestibule.clip measured a 0.045
# moulding at metre 32 and got nought voxels back, because a moulding is only the swell in FRONT of
# its core, so the two curved members here are given a plain box behind them.

let enf_g_skirt_w = box 13.90 1.75 -2.85   13.995 1.98 2.95 round=0.010
let enf_g_body_w  = box 13.90 1.98 -2.85   13.9725 2.565 2.95
# EVERY CURVED MEMBER IS GIVEN A CORE BOX BEHIND IT. salon.clip says why in one line — a moulding
# is only the swell in FRONT of its core, so drawn bare it is a ribbon floating off the wall — and
# this file measured it: with the four ovolos written bare, `--part enf_g_dado` at metre 32 came
# back as FOUR components, two of them a strip one voxel square and 185 voxels long running the
# whole length of the room at the outer arris. Nothing else in the report changed.
let enf_g_ovolo_w = ovolo 13.95 2.565 -2.85   13.995 2.655 2.95
let enf_g_ocore_w = box 13.90 2.565 -2.85   13.95 2.655 2.95
let enf_g_cap_w   = box 13.90 2.655 -2.85   13.995 2.70 2.95 round=0.008

let enf_g_skirt_e = box 15.055 1.75 -2.85   15.20 1.98 2.95 round=0.010
let enf_g_body_e  = box 15.0775 1.98 -2.85   15.20 2.565 2.95
let enf_g_ovolo_e = ovolo 15.10 2.565 -2.85   15.055 2.655 2.95
let enf_g_ocore_e = box 15.10 2.565 -2.85   15.20 2.655 2.95
let enf_g_cap_e   = box 15.055 2.655 -2.85   15.20 2.70 2.95 round=0.008

let enf_g_dado = union {
    enf_g_skirt_w enf_g_body_w enf_g_ovolo_w enf_g_ocore_w enf_g_cap_w
    enf_g_skirt_e enf_g_body_e enf_g_ovolo_e enf_g_ocore_e enf_g_cap_e
}

# The cornice, 4.50 to 4.725, on the window head line so that no light is crossed by it and so
# that it is the lintel of both archways at the same time.
let enf_g_cor_bed_w = union {
    cyma_reversa 13.95 4.50 -2.85   14.04 4.635 2.95
    box 13.90 4.50 -2.85   13.95 4.635 2.95
}
let enf_g_cor_cor_w = box 13.905 4.635 -2.85   14.04 4.725 2.95 round=0.008
let enf_g_cor_bed_e = union {
    cyma_reversa 15.10 4.50 -2.85   15.01 4.635 2.95
    box 15.10 4.50 -2.85   15.20 4.635 2.95
}
let enf_g_cor_cor_e = box 15.01 4.635 -2.85   15.145 4.725 2.95 round=0.008
let enf_g_cornice = union {
    enf_g_cor_bed_w enf_g_cor_cor_w enf_g_cor_bed_e enf_g_cor_cor_e
}

# The four gilt strips that divide the mirror wall, on the window jamb lines. 0.09 wide, 0.045
# proud, bedded 0.05 into the lining at 14.00 so that they are one solid with it.
let enf_g_strip_seed = box 13.90 2.70 -0.045   13.995 4.50 0.045
let enf_g_strips = union {
    translate { enf_g_strip_seed } 0 0 -0.675
    translate { enf_g_strip_seed } 0 0  0.675
    translate { enf_g_strip_seed } 0 0 -2.025
    translate { enf_g_strip_seed } 0 0  2.025
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE SHELVES — three runs, on corbels every M, and why they are M/4 deep
# ================================================================================================
#
# 0.09 thick and 0.09 (M/5) DEEP, from 13.90 — 0.05 into the lining — out to 13.99. A corbel every
# 0.45 under each of them, 0.10 wide and 0.135 tall and 0.045 proud.
#
# THEY WERE M/4 DEEP AND THE REASON WAS windows.clip's CUTTER; THEY ARE M/5 AND THE REASON IS THE
# WALKING BAND. The cutter has been fixed to 1.00 and lands at 15.00, so nothing on this wall is
# near it at any depth. What binds now is a person: the passage is 1.15 m in the clear and a shelf
# and a dado cap facing each other take a bite out of the middle of it at exactly the height a
# shoulder is. At M/4, with the vessels standing on it, the widest thing on this wall reached 14.067
# and the guaranteed clear band was 0.988 m — twelve millimetres under the brief's 1.00 for a
# passage. At M/5 the shelf front is 13.99, the widest vessel reaches 14.022, and `enf_walk_g` — a
# box 1.00 m wide and 2.10 m tall, run the whole length of the passage — measures EMPTY.

let enf_shelf_seed  = box 13.90 -0.09 -2.85   13.99 0 2.95 round=0.008
let enf_corbel_seed = box 13.90 -0.225 -0.05   13.995 -0.09 0.05 round=0.010
let enf_corbels_one = intersection {
    repeat { enf_corbel_seed } z=0.45 nz=6
    box 13.80 -0.30 -2.85   14.05 0 2.95
}
let enf_tier_seed = union { enf_shelf_seed enf_corbels_one }

let enf_tier_a = translate { enf_tier_seed } 0 2.925 0
let enf_tier_b = translate { enf_tier_seed } 0 3.375 0
let enf_tier_c = translate { enf_tier_seed } 0 3.825 0
let enf_shelves = union { enf_tier_a enf_tier_b enf_tier_c }

# ================================================================================================
# THE VESSELS — a jar every M/2 and a beaker between every two of them, on all three shelves
# ================================================================================================
#
# Drawn once at z = 0 with the shelf top at y = 0 and the foot bedded 0.045 into it, then repeated
# along the run and carried up to each tier. Every member overlaps the one under it IN RANGE and
# not merely at a plane — foot -0.045..0.025, belly 0.015..0.115, neck 0.095..0.175, lid
# 0.155..0.215 — because a union whose extents are contiguous is one solid whatever the voxel grid
# does with it, and a union that merely touches on a plane is two.
#
# NOTHING FREE-STANDING HERE IS UNDER 0.052 ACROSS, and the number went UP when the passage was
# widened. That is ballroom.clip's rule read properly — thin matter is safe when it is knitted
# along its whole length to what it lies on and it comes apart when it is a rod in mid air — plus
# one thing that rule does not say: WHERE a thin thing stands decides whether it exists at all.
# The vessel row moved 0.095 m west with the wall, and at metre 16 that put a 0.048 neck between
# two sample columns instead of on one: `--metre 16` went from TWO components to TWENTY, eighteen
# of them a one- or two-voxel neck or lid adrift over a belly that had survived. The neck is 0.072
# now and the lid 0.076, which is what it takes for the whole jar to be sampled at every z the row
# stands on; the thinnest free-standing thing left is a taper at 0.052.

let enf_jar_foot  = cylinder 13.970 -0.010 0  r=0.030 h=0.070 axis=y
let enf_jar_belly = sphere   13.970  0.065 0  r=0.052
let enf_jar_neck  = cylinder 13.970  0.140 0  r=0.036 h=0.100 axis=y
let enf_jar_lid   = sphere   13.970  0.195 0  r=0.038
let enf_jar = union { enf_jar_foot enf_jar_belly enf_jar_neck enf_jar_lid }

let enf_bkr_body = cylinder 13.975 0.030 0  r=0.030 h=0.150 axis=y
let enf_bkr_lip  = cylinder 13.975 0.110 0  r=0.036 h=0.040 axis=y
let enf_bkr = union { enf_bkr_body enf_bkr_lip }

# The two rows. Each is caught in a box it already fits inside, for the reason windows.clip gives
# under its fanlight rays: a repeat whose child's extent cannot be measured reports infinite bounds
# and no box in the clip can settle after it.
let enf_jars_one = intersection {
    repeat { enf_jar } z=0.225 nz=12
    box 13.90 -0.05 -2.80   14.03 0.25 2.80
}
let enf_bkrs_one = intersection {
    translate { repeat { enf_bkr } z=0.225 nz=11 } 0 0 0.1125
    box 13.90 -0.05 -2.80   14.02 0.14 2.80
}
let enf_vessels_row = union { enf_jars_one enf_bkrs_one }

let enf_vessels_a = translate { enf_vessels_row } 0 2.925 0
let enf_vessels_b = translate { enf_vessels_row } 0 3.375 0
let enf_vessels_c = translate { enf_vessels_row } 0 3.825 0
let enf_vessels = union { enf_vessels_a enf_vessels_b enf_vessels_c }

# The plates: six to a passage, standing on edge on the middle shelf against the mirror, which is
# the one place a 0.225 piece — the big end of the brief's range — will go in 0.90 m of room. A
# plate on edge needs 0.05 of depth and no more, so it can stand anywhere; these are on the two
# pier bays at z = +-0.90, +-1.35, +-1.80.
let enf_plate_seed = cylinder 13.935 3.450 0  r=0.1125 h=0.05 axis=x
let enf_plates = union {
    translate { enf_plate_seed } 0 0 -1.80
    translate { enf_plate_seed } 0 0 -1.35
    translate { enf_plate_seed } 0 0 -0.90
    translate { enf_plate_seed } 0 0  0.90
    translate { enf_plate_seed } 0 0  1.35
    translate { enf_plate_seed } 0 0  1.80
}

# THE FOUR BRACKET JARS, and the only things in this file that project M/2. They are on the EAST
# wall, on the two piers between the lights at z = 1.125 and 1.575 and their mirror images, which
# is the one band of the east wall no window reveal touches at any x. Everything else on that wall
# had to be kept behind 14.20 or cut back to a jamb.
# THEY STAND AT 4.05 AND NOT AT 3.15, AND THE HEAD PROBE IS WHY. At 3.15 the shelf was 1.26 m
# above the floor and 0.225 out into a passage 0.90 m wide, which is a shoulder at chest height:
# `--part enf_head_g` came back with 1028 voxels in it. At 4.05 — 2.25 m over the floor, 5 M above
# it, and the same line the girandoles opposite start on — the probe is empty and the jar still has
# 0.045 of daylight under the cornice. The corbel that carries it is kept to the dado cap's own
# 0.09 projection so that the part of the bracket inside the head band is no more proud than the
# wall it is on.
let enf_brk_shelf  = box 14.875 3.96 -0.18   15.20 4.05 0.18 round=0.010
let enf_brk_corbel = box 15.055 3.78 -0.09   15.20 3.96 0.09 round=0.012
let enf_brk_jar    = union {
    cylinder 14.99 4.08 0  r=0.055 h=0.12 axis=y
    sphere   14.99 4.20 0  r=0.090
    cylinder 14.99 4.32 0  r=0.042 h=0.12 axis=y
    sphere   14.99 4.40 0  r=0.055
}
let enf_bracket_one = union { enf_brk_shelf enf_brk_corbel enf_brk_jar }
let enf_brackets = union {
    translate { enf_bracket_one } 0 0 1.125
    translate { enf_bracket_one } 0 0 1.575
    translate { enf_bracket_one } 0 0 -1.125
    translate { enf_bracket_one } 0 0 -1.575
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE GIRANDOLES — six to a cabinet, in the band between the top shelf and the cornice
# ================================================================================================
#
# A mirrored backplate on the mirror wall, two ormolu branches, two wax tapers and two flames. They
# stand at z = +-0.45, +-1.35 and +-2.25, which is the 0.90 (2 M) grid measured off the middle
# window's axis, so two of them flank each light and one stands on each pier. The whole fitting reaches x = 14.185 and no further, 0.015 clear of windows.clip's
# reveal line, which is why the branches are short.
#
# Everything overlaps in range: backplate to 14.095, branch capsule from 14.06, taper from y 4.20,
# flame from 4.34 against a taper head at 4.38.

let enf_gir_plate = box 13.90 4.05 -0.1125   13.995 4.41 0.1125 round=0.010
let enf_gir_arm_a = capsule 13.96 4.14 0   14.015 4.23 -0.16 r=0.026
let enf_gir_arm_b = capsule 13.96 4.14 0   14.015 4.23  0.16 r=0.026
let enf_gir_tap_a = cylinder 14.015 4.29 -0.16 r=0.026 h=0.18 axis=y
let enf_gir_tap_b = cylinder 14.015 4.29  0.16 r=0.026 h=0.18 axis=y
let enf_gir_fla_a = ellipsoid 14.015 4.40 -0.16 rx=0.030 ry=0.060 rz=0.030
let enf_gir_fla_b = ellipsoid 14.015 4.40  0.16 rx=0.030 ry=0.060 rz=0.030

let enf_gir_metal = union { enf_gir_plate enf_gir_arm_a enf_gir_arm_b }
let enf_gir_wax   = union { enf_gir_tap_a enf_gir_tap_b }
let enf_gir_fire  = union { enf_gir_fla_a enf_gir_fla_b }
let enf_gir_one   = union { enf_gir_metal enf_gir_wax enf_gir_fire }

let enf_gir_place_a = translate { enf_gir_one } 0 0 -2.25
let enf_gir_place_b = translate { enf_gir_one } 0 0 -1.35
let enf_gir_place_c = translate { enf_gir_one } 0 0 -0.45
let enf_gir_place_d = translate { enf_gir_one } 0 0  0.45
let enf_gir_place_e = translate { enf_gir_one } 0 0  1.35
let enf_gir_place_f = translate { enf_gir_one } 0 0  2.25
let enf_girandoles = union {
    enf_gir_place_a enf_gir_place_b enf_gir_place_c
    enf_gir_place_d enf_gir_place_e enf_gir_place_f
}

# The metal, the wax and the flame of all six, as three zones, so each can be painted with a rule
# that names a shape of its own rather than a coordinate.
let enf_gir_metal_all = union {
    translate { enf_gir_metal } 0 0 -2.25   translate { enf_gir_metal } 0 0 -1.35
    translate { enf_gir_metal } 0 0 -0.45   translate { enf_gir_metal } 0 0  0.45
    translate { enf_gir_metal } 0 0  1.35   translate { enf_gir_metal } 0 0  2.25
}
let enf_gir_wax_all = union {
    translate { enf_gir_wax } 0 0 -2.25   translate { enf_gir_wax } 0 0 -1.35
    translate { enf_gir_wax } 0 0 -0.45   translate { enf_gir_wax } 0 0  0.45
    translate { enf_gir_wax } 0 0  1.35   translate { enf_gir_wax } 0 0  2.25
}
let enf_gir_fire_all = union {
    translate { enf_gir_fire } 0 0 -2.25   translate { enf_gir_fire } 0 0 -1.35
    translate { enf_gir_fire } 0 0 -0.45   translate { enf_gir_fire } 0 0  0.45
    translate { enf_gir_fire } 0 0  1.35   translate { enf_gir_fire } 0 0  2.25
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE TRIM OF THE GALLERY — base course, picture frames, impost
# ================================================================================================
#
# The base course is M tall and 0.09 proud, and on the EAST wall it is CUT BACK TO THE WINDOW
# JAMBS by this file. windows.clip's piano-nobile opening starts at the floor, so a base course
# run straight through would be taken out from 14.20 eastwards by the manifest and would end
# wherever that difference happened to leave it. Cut here, it returns cleanly on each jamb.

# 0.045 PROUD AND NOT 0.09, AND `enf_walk_u` IS WHY. At 0.09 the cap reached 15.01 and 14.04, and
# the 1.00 m band came back with 200 voxels of the east cap in it — a moulding 0.36 m over the floor
# standing in the middle of a passage 1.15 m wide. The body is flush with the wall now and the cap
# is the only member that projects, which is what a base course is anyway.
let enf_u_base_w  = box 13.90 6.30 -2.85   13.95 6.66 2.95 round=0.010
let enf_u_bcap_w  = ovolo 13.95 6.66 -2.85   13.995 6.75 2.95
let enf_u_bcore_w = box 13.90 6.66 -2.85   13.95 6.75 2.95
let enf_u_base_e_mass = box 15.10 6.30 -2.85   15.20 6.66 2.95 round=0.010
let enf_u_bcap_e_mass = union {
    ovolo 15.10 6.66 -2.85   15.055 6.75 2.95
    box 15.10 6.66 -2.85   15.20 6.75 2.95
}

# The jamb cutter: windows.clip's three openings, 0.045 wider each side so the return stands clear
# of the reveal rather than exactly on it.
let enf_win_z_seed = box 14.95 6.20 -0.72   15.35 8.60 0.72
let enf_win_zone = union {
    translate { enf_win_z_seed } 0 0 -2.70
    enf_win_z_seed
    translate { enf_win_z_seed } 0 0  2.70
}
let enf_u_base_e = difference { union { enf_u_base_e_mass enf_u_bcap_e_mass } enf_win_zone }
let enf_u_base = union { enf_u_base_w enf_u_bcap_w enf_u_bcore_w enf_u_base_e }

# The gilt fillet round every picture: a frame 0.09 wide standing 0.045 proud, drawn as the
# difference of two boxes so all four mitres are free.
let enf_frm_w_seed = difference {
    box 13.90 6.885 -0.27   13.995 7.965 0.27
    box 13.90 6.975 -0.18   14.10  7.875 0.18
}
let enf_frames_w = union {
    translate { enf_frm_w_seed } 0 0 -0.675
    translate { enf_frm_w_seed } 0 0  0.675
    translate { enf_frm_w_seed } 0 0 -2.025
    translate { enf_frm_w_seed } 0 0  2.025
}
let enf_frm_e_seed = difference {
    box 15.055 6.885 -0.54   15.20 7.965 0.54
    box 14.95  6.975 -0.45   15.20 7.875 0.45
}
let enf_frames_e = union {
    translate { enf_frm_e_seed } 0 0 -1.35
    translate { enf_frm_e_seed } 0 0  1.35
}
let enf_frames = union { enf_frames_w enf_frames_e }

# The impost, 7.92 to 8.10, the line the vault springs off. On the east wall it is cut by the same
# jamb cutter, for the same reason as the base course.
# 8.46 to 8.55 AND NOT 7.92 TO 8.10. It has to sit under the vault's own springing, and it has to
# stand clear of the head band: a member 0.09 proud at 8.10 is 1.80 m over the floor, which is
# halls.clip's exact mistake — "it was then at head height" — and `--part enf_head_u` finds it.
let enf_imp_w = union {
    ovolo 13.95 8.685 -2.85   13.995 8.775 2.95
    box 13.90 8.685 -2.85   13.95 8.775 2.95
}
let enf_imp_e = union {
    ovolo 15.10 8.685 -2.85   15.055 8.775 2.95
    box 15.10 8.685 -2.85   15.20 8.775 2.95
}
let enf_impost = union { enf_imp_w enf_imp_e }

# ================================================================================================
# THE SOCLES AND THE BUSTS
# ================================================================================================
#
# One to a niche, five to a gallery, and they alternate in BOTH material pairs down the run:
# alabaster on giallo at z = 0 and +-2.70, marble on rosso at z = +-1.35. `alabaster` is
# translucent=210 and `marble` is 110, so the row is the subsurface question asked twice, a metre
# and a half apart, under the same window.
#
# Each socle is 0.27 wide and reaches back to 13.855 — past the niche's own back at 13.87 where it
# meets the side of the hollow — so it is bedded in the wall rather than standing on a plane. Each
# bust is bedded 0.035 into its socle and its head crowns at 7.64, a centimetre under the niche's
# springing at 7.65.

let enf_socle_seed = box 13.700 6.75 -0.135   13.95 7.20 0.135 round=0.010
let enf_bust_seed = union {
    ellipsoid 13.88 7.30 0  rx=0.085 ry=0.150 rz=0.115
    cylinder  13.88 7.45 0  r=0.050 h=0.16 axis=y
    sphere    13.88 7.52 0  r=0.095
}

let enf_socle_a = union {
    enf_socle_seed
    translate { enf_socle_seed } 0 0 -2.70
    translate { enf_socle_seed } 0 0  2.70
}
let enf_socle_b = union {
    translate { enf_socle_seed } 0 0 -1.35
    translate { enf_socle_seed } 0 0  1.35
}
let enf_bust_a = union {
    enf_bust_seed
    translate { enf_bust_seed } 0 0 -2.70
    translate { enf_bust_seed } 0 0  2.70
}
let enf_bust_b = union {
    translate { enf_bust_seed } 0 0 -1.35
    translate { enf_bust_seed } 0 0  1.35
}
let enf_sculpture = union { enf_socle_a enf_socle_b enf_bust_a enf_bust_b }

# ================================================================================================
# THE SCONCES — eight to a gallery, at eye height, between a niche and a frame
# ================================================================================================
#
# The niches are 0.45 wide on 1.35 centres and the frames 0.54 over all between them, which leaves
# eight strips of plain wall 0.18 across, and a sconce stands in the middle of each: z = +-0.315,
# +-1.035, +-1.665 and +-2.385, every one of them a multiple of M/10, and none of them a number
# anybody chose — each is the middle of what a niche and a frame left. Their flames are at 8.11,
# which is 1.81 m over the floor: the height a picture is lit from.
#
# The whole fitting reaches x = 14.185, the same 0.015 clear of the reveal line as the girandoles
# below, and for the same reason: at z = +-0.315 and +-2.385 a sconce stands squarely inside a
# window's own z band and anything past 14.20 there is deleted by the manifest.

let enf_scn_plate = box 13.90 7.65 -0.08   13.995 8.10 0.08 round=0.010
let enf_scn_arm   = capsule 13.96 7.74 0   14.015 7.86 0 r=0.026
let enf_scn_tap   = cylinder 14.015 7.96 0 r=0.026 h=0.22 axis=y
let enf_scn_fla   = ellipsoid 14.015 8.11 0 rx=0.030 ry=0.060 rz=0.030

let enf_scn_metal = union { enf_scn_plate enf_scn_arm }
let enf_scn_wax   = enf_scn_tap
let enf_scn_fire  = enf_scn_fla

let enf_scn_metal_all = union {
    translate { enf_scn_metal } 0 0 -2.385   translate { enf_scn_metal } 0 0 -1.665
    translate { enf_scn_metal } 0 0 -1.035   translate { enf_scn_metal } 0 0 -0.315
    translate { enf_scn_metal } 0 0  0.315   translate { enf_scn_metal } 0 0  1.035
    translate { enf_scn_metal } 0 0  1.665   translate { enf_scn_metal } 0 0  2.385
}
let enf_scn_wax_all = union {
    translate { enf_scn_wax } 0 0 -2.385   translate { enf_scn_wax } 0 0 -1.665
    translate { enf_scn_wax } 0 0 -1.035   translate { enf_scn_wax } 0 0 -0.315
    translate { enf_scn_wax } 0 0  0.315   translate { enf_scn_wax } 0 0  1.035
    translate { enf_scn_wax } 0 0  1.665   translate { enf_scn_wax } 0 0  2.385
}
let enf_scn_fire_all = union {
    translate { enf_scn_fire } 0 0 -2.385   translate { enf_scn_fire } 0 0 -1.665
    translate { enf_scn_fire } 0 0 -1.035   translate { enf_scn_fire } 0 0 -0.315
    translate { enf_scn_fire } 0 0  0.315   translate { enf_scn_fire } 0 0  1.035
    translate { enf_scn_fire } 0 0  1.665   translate { enf_scn_fire } 0 0  2.385
}
let enf_sconces = union { enf_scn_metal_all enf_scn_wax_all enf_scn_fire_all }

# ================================================================================================
# THE PART
# ================================================================================================
#
# Everything that STANDS in the air is cut by the four doorway boxes before it is added, exactly as
# halls.clip and salon.clip cut theirs — a member does not stop at the wall face, it projects into
# the room, and a void that stops at the face cuts the wall and leaves the trim standing across the
# opening. The runs already stop 0.10 short of each end face; this is the belt as well as the
# braces, and it costs nothing.

let enf_trim = difference {
    union {
        enf_g_dado enf_g_cornice enf_g_strips enf_shelves
        enf_u_base enf_frames enf_impost enf_architraves
    }
    enf_dr_gs enf_dr_gn enf_dr_us enf_dr_un
}

let enf_fittings = union {
    enf_vessels enf_plates enf_brackets enf_girandoles enf_sculpture enf_sconces
}

let enf_east = union { enf_shell enf_trim enf_fittings }

# BUILT ENTIRELY ON POSITIVE x AND FOLDED, and that is not a preference. `mirror` folds the
# coordinate to its ABSOLUTE value, so what it shows on both sides is whatever the child has on the
# POSITIVE side; a child drawn on -x is asked about at +|x|, finds nothing, and disappears with no
# error, `components 1` and an empty `never fired` list. It ate six sconces in salon.clip and a set
# of desk legs in library.clip twice. Every coordinate above is positive; the west copy is checked
# by `enf_west_check` below, which must report matter.
let part_enfilade = mirror { enf_east } axis=x

# WHAT THIS PART REMOVES FROM THE BUILDING: the air of the two passages, their doorways, and the
# five niches, LESS every stone of mine standing in any of it.
#
# Written as a difference against my own mass and not as a list of boxes, for the reason the
# manifest's own note gives at length: six girandoles, eight sconces, twenty-eight flames, five
# busts and about three hundred vessels stand in this air, and a void written as a bare box would
# cut every one of them away and leave nothing behind but the 0.045 of backplate buried in the wall.
#
# THE NICHES ARE IN IT AND THEY HAVE TO BE. They are cut west of 14.05, and halls.clip's block is
# solid to 13.95 and up to 6.975 — which is 0.225 ABOVE the gallery floor — so the bottom of every
# niche at z within +-2.925 would be filled with the hall's stone if this shape stopped at my own
# west face. The rebates and the sunk panels are NOT in it: they are cut out of my own block above
# and the manifest never has to know they exist, so they cannot open somebody else's wall.
let enfilade_air = mirror { enf_air } axis=x
let void_enfilade = difference { enfilade_air part_enfilade }

# ================================================================================================
# THE WALKING BAND, AS A SHAPE, BECAUSE A WIDTH TAKEN AT ONE POINT IS NOT A WIDTH
# ================================================================================================
#
# `--gap x@y,z` CANNOT MEASURE THIS PASSAGE ON THE ASSEMBLED BUILDING, and that is worth writing
# down because it looks as if it should. `--gap` reports the LONGEST unbroken run of air on its
# line; every line along x through this building at any height a person stands at crosses the
# rotunda and the halls before it gets here, so the answer comes back 25.750 m and it is the
# rotunda's. Asked on the fragment probe instead, the longest run is the 27 m of empty box between
# the east passage and the west one. There is no line that answers the question.
#
# So the width is built as a SHAPE, which is what BRIEF.md rule 7 says to do for anything a
# heightfield cannot walk: the volume a person sweeps, intersected with this part, WHICH MUST
# MEASURE EMPTY. 1.00 m wide by 2.10 m tall, run the whole 6.05 m length of each passage, on both
# floors:
#
#   ./build/clipcheck clips/facility/requests/enfilade-probe.clip --metre 32 --part enf_walk_g
#   ./build/clipcheck clips/facility/requests/enfilade-probe.clip --metre 32 --part enf_walk_u
#
# 14.05 .. 15.05 IS WHERE THE 1.00 CAN GO AND THERE IS NOTHING SPARE AT EITHER END. On the west the
# widest thing is a jar's belly at 14.022 and a sconce's flame at 14.045; on the east the dado cap,
# the skirting, the base course, the architrave sill and the bracket corbel all stand at 15.055.
# Move any one of them 6 mm and this stops being true.
let enf_walk_g = intersection { part_enfilade  box 14.05 1.80 -2.90   15.05 3.90 3.05 }
let enf_walk_u = intersection { part_enfilade  box 14.05 6.30 -2.90   15.05 8.40 3.05 }

# The check that the fold actually happened. `--part enf_west_check` must report matter; if the
# west copy were lost it would report an empty clip and nothing else in any measurement would
# change.
let enf_west_check = intersection {
    part_enfilade
    box -15.20 1.70 -3.05   -13.55 9.95 3.15
}

# ================================================================================================
# PAINT
# ================================================================================================
#
# Later coats cover earlier ones: shell, then floors, then the linings, then the mirror, then the
# metal and the fire on top of everything.
#
# EVERY ZONE IS ONE OF MY OWN SHAPES FOLDED TO BOTH SIDES, so the west passage is painted by the
# same rules as the east and the two cannot drift.
#
# `below=0.035` AND NOT 0.02 ON EVERYTHING PLACED BY A TRANSFORM. This was found while building the
# ballroom and BRIEF.md has not caught up: a voxel is decided solid by coverage and painted by a
# rule tested at its centre, so anything placed by `translate`, `rotate` or `repeat` leaves 3 to
# 16% of its own solid unpainted at 0.02 — the ballroom's ceiling trellis came back with 1021
# limestone voxels strung along every rib, silently. Everything below that came out of a repeat or
# a translate is at 0.035; the plain boxes and mouldings are at 0.02, which is still comfortably
# past the manifest's 12 mm of grain. Never 0.

let enf_face = mirror { enf_shell } axis=x
paint plaster where=enf_face below=0.02

# The two rooms' linings. The cabinet is boiserie and the gallery is gesso, which is eight values
# apart and thirty rougher — the difference between a panelled closet and a plastered gallery said
# in material rather than in geometry, because there is no room in 0.90 m for geometry to say it.
let enf_g_lining = mirror {
    intersection { enf_shell  box 13.75 1.70 -3.00   15.25 4.50 3.15 }
} axis=x
paint boiserie where=enf_g_lining below=0.02

let enf_u_lining = mirror {
    intersection { enf_shell  box 13.55 6.30 -3.00   15.25 8.775 3.15 }
} axis=x
paint gesso where=enf_u_lining below=0.02

# --- the two ceilings ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Both plaster, and both are surfaces with no direct light on any of them: the cove is above the
# window head and the vault springs 1.80 m above the gallery floor, so everything either of them
# shows is bounce. That is the same test the ballroom's cove is for, in a room a tenth the width.
# The zones are the shell caught in a band, and not the air grown by a round: `round` on a shape
# whose extent is a union can come apart, and a zone that has to reach every voxel LINING a coved
# surface is more certainly written as the solid itself over the band the surface runs in.
let enf_cove_face = mirror {
    intersection { enf_shell  box 13.90 4.50 -3.00   15.25 5.00 3.15 }
} axis=x
paint plaster where=enf_cove_face below=0.02

let enf_vault_face = mirror {
    intersection { enf_shell  box 13.90 8.775 -3.00   15.25 9.40 3.15 }
} axis=x
paint plaster where=enf_vault_face below=0.02

# --- the cabinet floor: parquet with a walnut border ---------------------------------------------
#
# Thin slabs straddling the floor plane at 1.80 so they catch its surface and nothing under it.
let enf_g_fl_zone = mirror { box 13.95 1.70 -2.95   15.10 1.86 3.10 } axis=x
paint parquet where=enf_g_fl_zone below=0.02

let enf_g_fl_field = box 14.085 1.60 -2.815   14.965 1.96 2.965
let enf_g_fl_bord  = mirror {
    difference { box 13.95 1.70 -2.95   15.10 1.86 3.10   enf_g_fl_field }
} axis=x
paint walnut where=enf_g_fl_bord below=0.02

# --- the gallery floor: travertine with a running lozenge of granite ------------------------------
#
# Thirteen lozenges on M centres down the middle of the run, each a square of 0.45 turned an eighth
# of a turn and caught in a box it already fits inside — a rotate is deliberately given no bounding
# box by the field, and a repeat over a child whose extent cannot be measured makes every box in
# the clip unsettleable. halls.clip's floor lozenge is written exactly this way.
let enf_u_fl_zone = mirror { box 13.95 6.20 -2.95   15.10 6.36 3.10 } axis=x
paint travertine where=enf_u_fl_zone below=0.02

let enf_loz_one = intersection {
    rotate { box -0.16 6.20 -0.16   0.16 6.36 0.16 } y=0.125
    box -0.24 6.20 -0.24   0.24 6.36 0.24
}
let enf_lozenges = mirror {
    intersection {
        translate { repeat { enf_loz_one } z=0.45 nz=6 } 14.525 0 0
        box 14.225 6.20 -2.80   14.825 6.36 2.80
    }
} axis=x
paint granite where=enf_lozenges below=0.035

# --- the dado, and the one lacquer in the building ------------------------------------------------
let enf_dado_face = mirror { enf_g_dado } axis=x
paint lacquer_red where=enf_dado_face below=0.02

# --- THE MIRROR WALL ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The whole west face of the cabinet between the dado cap at 2.70 and the cornice soffit at 4.50,
# painted on the lining itself rather than on a plate sunk into it. A sunk plate would have wanted
# the shelves to stand 0.03 clear of the ground they are bedded in, and a shelf that touches its
# wall on a plane is a shelf that is not attached to it.
#
# The zone is a thin slab straddling that face, so it catches the face and not the 0.15 of lining
# behind it — and it is `intersection`ed with the shell, so it cannot reach the east wall 0.90 m
# away, which a bare box at this width very nearly would.
let enf_mirror_face = mirror {
    intersection { enf_shell  box 13.90 2.70 -2.95   13.99 4.50 3.10 }
} axis=x
paint mirror where=enf_mirror_face below=0.02

let enf_strip_face = mirror { enf_g_strips } axis=x
paint gold_leaf where=enf_strip_face below=0.02

let enf_cornice_face = mirror { enf_g_cornice } axis=x
paint gesso where=enf_cornice_face below=0.02

# --- the shelves and what stands on them ------------------------------------------------------------
let enf_shelf_face = mirror { enf_shelves } axis=x
paint ormolu where=enf_shelf_face below=0.035

let enf_jar_face = mirror {
    union {
        translate { enf_jars_one } 0 2.925 0
        translate { enf_jars_one } 0 3.375 0
        translate { enf_jars_one } 0 3.825 0
    }
} axis=x
paint porcelain where=enf_jar_face below=0.035

let enf_bkr_face = mirror {
    union {
        translate { enf_bkrs_one } 0 2.925 0
        translate { enf_bkrs_one } 0 3.375 0
        translate { enf_bkrs_one } 0 3.825 0
    }
} axis=x
paint faience where=enf_bkr_face below=0.035

let enf_plate_face = mirror { enf_plates } axis=x
paint porcelain where=enf_plate_face below=0.035

let enf_brk_metal = mirror {
    union {
        translate { union { enf_brk_shelf enf_brk_corbel } } 0 0  1.125
        translate { union { enf_brk_shelf enf_brk_corbel } } 0 0  1.575
        translate { union { enf_brk_shelf enf_brk_corbel } } 0 0 -1.125
        translate { union { enf_brk_shelf enf_brk_corbel } } 0 0 -1.575
    }
} axis=x
paint ormolu where=enf_brk_metal below=0.035

let enf_brk_pots = mirror {
    union {
        translate { enf_brk_jar } 0 0  1.125   translate { enf_brk_jar } 0 0  1.575
        translate { enf_brk_jar } 0 0 -1.125   translate { enf_brk_jar } 0 0 -1.575
    }
} axis=x
paint faience where=enf_brk_pots below=0.035

# --- the gallery's linings, hangings and stone --------------------------------------------------------
let enf_base_face = mirror { enf_u_base } axis=x
paint marble where=enf_base_face below=0.02

let enf_imp_face = mirror { enf_impost } axis=x
paint marble where=enf_imp_face below=0.02

# The window architraves and the gallery doorcase. Marble, like every other dressed stone member in
# this passage, and NOT gesso: an architrave is the piece of the window that is inside the room, and
# it should read as the same stone the base course and the impost are.
let enf_arc_face = mirror { enf_architraves } axis=x
paint marble where=enf_arc_face below=0.035

# The niches. The hollow itself is grown enough to catch the voxels that line it rather than the air
# in it, and it is painted a stop warmer than the gallery wall so the bust in front of it has a
# ground to be seen against — halls.clip put porphyry behind its statue for exactly this reason and
# rotunda.clip a verde lining behind a gilt urn.
let enf_niche_face = mirror { round { enf_niches } by=0.03 } axis=x
paint travertine where=enf_niche_face below=0.035

# The hangings that stand in for canvases: damask on the four west panels and velvet on the two
# east ones. damask is 118,32,44 at rough=246 and velvet 58,22,58 at rough=252 with sheen 14 — the
# retro-reflective end of the range, brightest where you look along the light, and the exact
# opposite failure mode from the mirror one storey down.
let enf_pan_w_face = mirror { round { enf_pan_w } by=0.03 } axis=x
paint damask where=enf_pan_w_face below=0.035

let enf_pan_e_face = mirror { round { enf_pan_e } by=0.03 } axis=x
paint velvet where=enf_pan_e_face below=0.035

let enf_frame_face = mirror { enf_frames } axis=x
paint gilt where=enf_frame_face below=0.035

# The busts and their socles, alternating down the run.
let enf_socle_a_face = mirror { enf_socle_a } axis=x
paint giallo where=enf_socle_a_face below=0.035

let enf_socle_b_face = mirror { enf_socle_b } axis=x
paint rosso where=enf_socle_b_face below=0.035

let enf_bust_a_face = mirror { enf_bust_a } axis=x
paint alabaster where=enf_bust_a_face below=0.035

let enf_bust_b_face = mirror { enf_bust_b } axis=x
paint marble where=enf_bust_b_face below=0.035

# --- the metal, the wax and the fire, last, over everything ---------------------------------------
let enf_metal_face = mirror { union { enf_gir_metal_all enf_scn_metal_all } } axis=x
paint ormolu where=enf_metal_face below=0.035

let enf_wax_face = mirror { union { enf_gir_wax_all enf_scn_wax_all } } axis=x
paint wax where=enf_wax_face below=0.035

let enf_fire_face = mirror { union { enf_gir_fire_all enf_scn_fire_all } } axis=x
paint taper where=enf_fire_face below=0.035
